// 🔢 Problem: 263A – Beautiful Matrix

// Today’s problem is a simple introduction to 2D arrays, matrix traversal, and calculating row/column differences. The goal is to move the single 1 in a 5 × 5 matrix to the center position (3, 3) using the minimum number of row and column swaps.

// 💡 Approach --->

// Read the 5 × 5 matrix.
// Traverse through every element of the matrix.
// Find the position of the single 1 by storing its row and column.
// The required middle position is row 3, column 3.
// Calculate the number of row moves needed using the difference between the current row and row 3.
// Calculate the number of column moves needed using the difference between the current column and column 3.
// Add both differences to get the minimum number of moves.
// Print the final answer.


import java.util.Scanner;
public class Matrix{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int swap = 0;
        int arr[][] = new int[5][5];
        for(int i = 0;i<5;i++){
            for(int j =0;j<5;j++){
                arr[i][j]=sc.nextInt();
                if(arr[i][j]==1){
                    swap = Math.abs(i-2)+Math.abs(j-2);
                   
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.print(swap);
    }
}
